To fix the issue where the jQuery code adds all classes to all fields instead of adding a specific class to each field instance, you can indeed use `.each()` to iterate over each field element individually. 

Here’s an adapted version of your code that incorporates `.each()` to apply the class specifically to each instance of the field:

```javascript
Drupal.behaviors.burgerRating = {
    attach: function (context, settings) {
        $('.field-name-field-rating2', context).each(function() {
            var burgerRating = $(this).text(); // Use `$(this)` to get the text of the current element
            $(this).addClass(burgerRating); // Add the class to the current element
        });
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
- **`.each()`**: The function iterates over each matched element in the jQuery object, allowing you to apply logic to each field instance individually.
- **`$(this)`**: This refers to the current element in the iteration, ensuring that you add the class based on the text of that specific field instance, rather than the collective text of all matched elements.

By implementing this change, each field will receive a class corresponding to its specific rating value, resulting in the desired output structure:

```html
<div class="field-name-field-rating2 rating-1">
<div class="field-name-field-rating2 rating-5">
<div class="field-name-field-rating2 rating-8">
```

